{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000613","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 20 candidates are direct matches for urinary bladder cancer (trait_reported is bladder cancer and trait_efo aligns to urinary bladder carcinoma/cancer; phenotyping_reported is generally bladder cancer / cancer of bladder / incident bladder cancer, with one urothelial carcinoma endpoint). No model reports PRS-only AUROC/R2, so full-model AUROC is not a reliable cross-model discriminator (and varies with covariates), making selection inherently limited.

Within the most internally consistent and directly aligned set (Cancer PRSweb bladder cancer scores), PGS000613 (P+T, 15 variants; UKB-trained) shows among the strongest visible PRS-specific evidence via effect size (OR per SD 1.301, 95% CI 1.227–1.379) and a strong top-1% vs rest OR (2.91, 95% CI 1.99–4.24), with a similar full-model AUROC to its closest sibling PGS000611 (0.571 vs 0.567) and a slightly stronger top-1% signal. This makes PGS000613 the best-supported direct-match choice given the available PRS-relevant metrics.

Main limitation: the pool contains other strong direct-match candidates with large validations and time-to-event HRs (e.g., PGS000071/PGS000782) and several models have only full-model AUROC or limited metrics; because PRS-only metrics are absent and covariate sets differ (some include smoking/BMI), the evidence is insufficient to make a high-confidence, clearly dominant selection."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000541","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates for the target EFO "uterine cancer" include PGS000541 and PGS000542 (both "Malignant neoplasm of uterus" with phenotyping_reported matching the target) and also PGS001795 ("Uterine cancer" but with trait_efo "uterine carcinoma"). Endometrial-cancer models (e.g., PGS000075, PGS003381) are related and potentially dominant-subtype matches per domain guidance, but they are not exact uterine-cancer endpoints and therefore are weaker as direct-match for this stage.

Among the exact uterine-cancer endpoint models, PGS000541 and PGS000542 are near-clones from the same publication family (Cancer PRSweb), evaluated in EUR with basic covariates (age, sex, batch, PCs). Their reported discrimination is similar (full-model AUROC 0.576 vs 0.572), but PGS000542 shows a higher top-1% vs rest OR (2.6 vs 2.12), while PGS000541 has a slightly higher AUROC; neither provides PRS-only AUROC/R2, so the full-model AUROC cannot be treated as a PRS-comparable metric. Given this near-tie and lack of PRS-only metrics, selection is uncertain; I choose PGS000541 as marginally preferred based on slightly higher reported AUROC with otherwise comparable design.

Overall quality is sub-optimal because the best direct-match options have only modest full-model AUROC and no explicit PRS-only discrimination metrics, and a competing direct-label uterine cancer PRS-CS-auto model (PGS001795) comes from a global biobank framework context (per publication title) with very small covariates-regressed-out R2 (0.001948), making the ranking between these families not decisively supported by comparable PRS-only evidence."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000903","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 11 candidates are direct matches at the disease-concept level (trait_efo=Parkinson disease). However, several are endpoint-mismatched for generic Parkinson disease risk because they target progression/dementia outcomes (PGS000056 cognitive decline, PGS000211 motor decline, PGS000777 Parkinson’s disease dementia), so they are weaker for the target trait.

Among the remaining Parkinson’s disease status/incident candidates, PGS000903 (Lancet Neurol 2021 meta-analysis) has strong supportive evidence versus peers: it is explicitly for Parkinson’s disease, uses a higher-capacity PRS construction (C+T with 1,805 variants vs small GWAS-hit sets), reports the strongest available genetic risk signal (OR top vs bottom quartile 6.25, 95% CI 4.26–9.28) and includes an R² estimate (0.054) alongside a full-model AUROC (0.692). Its covariates are basic (PCs, age, sex), avoiding the heavier clinical-risk packaging seen in some alternatives (e.g., PGS001774 includes family history and multiple risk factors; PGS000750 includes family history and singleton LoF count).

Main limitation: the reported AUROC/R² appear to be from a full model rather than explicitly PGS-only, and evaluation ancestry is mixed rather than cleanly EUR-only; several other PD candidates also lack clearly PRS-only metrics, so while PGS000903 is best-supported overall, the evidence does not support a high-confidence, unambiguous winner."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS005254","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Among the 8 retrieved models, only three are direct matches to the target disease endpoint “aortic stenosis” (PGS005252, PGS005254, PGS005255/PGS005256 are aortic valve function traits but still use incident aortic stenosis phenotyping). The hypertrophic cardiomyopathy models (PGS000739, PGS000778, PGS004910, PGS004911) are not direct matches by trait/phenotype.

Within the aortic-stenosis–aligned set, PGS005254 is preferred because it is an incident aortic stenosis score with an explicit per-SD effect size (HR=1.64, 95% CI 1.50–1.78) and uses only basic demographic/PC covariates, making its reported association more comparable to PRS contribution than a heavily clinically adjusted full model. PGS005252 reports a high full-model C-index (0.87) but includes a broad bundle of clinical covariates (e.g., diabetes, CAD, hyperlipidemia, BMI, smoking, renal failure), and lacks PRS-only metrics; per the provided domain guidance, such full-model discrimination is discounted as potentially covariate-driven. The remaining two models (PGS005255/PGS005256) have different trait_reported labels (mean pressure gradient/peak aortic velocity) even though their phenotyping is also incident aortic stenosis; with similar HR (~1.53) and similarly sparse metadata, they are less directly aligned than the exact aortic stenosis trait_reported models.

Main limitation: none of the aortic stenosis candidates provide PRS-only AUROC/R², so the choice relies on endpoint alignment plus effect size and covariate comparability rather than directly comparable PRS discrimination metrics."}

